A||||2||88formula||Cranes comp-u-cam?||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||01-05-2000||10:12 PM||||Who has used Cranes comp-u-cam and how does it work? Which version did you use (theirs two?) I'm considering putting one in my Formula but I want to know from you experienced fiero people what to look for first.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-05-2000||11:48 PM||||If these are the same two cams they were offering a few years back and the only difference is the lobe centers then the choice is 107 or 112 degree lobe centers.  the 107 will pass emissions and is supposed to have better low end torque for automatics.  I purchased the 112 degree cam and it has more low end torque than stock, but at 4000 rpm it starts pulling hard.  Its rated at about 160 hp with cast iron head and stock exhaust.  I use pocket ported aluminum heads, chromoly 1.52:1 roller rockers, stiff springs, and large diameter exhaust and seem to get closer to closer to 190 hp.  The engine runs smooth, no lope or flat spots.  Cam specs are: in .427 lift, 204 degree duration. ex .454 lift, 216 duration.  112 degree lobe centers.  I've eaten two clutches in less than twenty k miles but thats probably me more than the cam.  I doubt that three degrees on lobe centers would drasticly affect performance but not having talked to anyone running the emissions version I only have cranes word for performance.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||01-06-2000||12:37 PM||||How is it possible to use aluminum heads?  Where did you get them?  Are you using the same intake?  I always thought it was impossible to put aluminum heads in our cars.||||reg||1
